WEBVTT

00:00:00.411 --> 00:00:04.630
In this video, you will learn how to find holiday lights and events near you.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:11.237
We will cover the season buttons, searching by city, getting driving routes, and narrowing the list with filters.

00:00:11.237 --> 00:00:12.215
We start on the map.

00:00:13.181 --> 00:00:17.525
To begin, open your web browser, and go to SoCal Lights dot app.

00:00:17.525 --> 00:00:19.386
That is the only address you need.

00:00:22.168 --> 00:00:24.588
This is the SoCal Lights home page.

00:00:24.588 --> 00:00:27.890
The first time you visit, it asks to use your location.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.447
Tap Use my location.

00:00:31.323 --> 00:00:35.342
That is how the map shows how far things are, and gives you directions.

00:00:35.342 --> 00:00:38.425
Your browser asks permission as well, so tap Allow.

00:00:40.169 --> 00:00:45.575
Your location is used only on your device, and you can say no at any time.

00:00:45.575 --> 00:00:47.668
The map zooms to where you are.

00:00:47.668 --> 00:00:51.594
There are no events on your street, so tap Show all of Southern California.

00:00:53.391 --> 00:00:58.183
Now every place shows how far away it is, nearest first, so you can plan your drive.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:04.072
The map shows different things at different times of the year, so first choose a season.

00:01:04.072 --> 00:01:06.072
Tap the season button at the top.

00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:09.885
It shows what is on right now.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.298
Pick the one you want.

00:01:12.298 --> 00:01:16.198
Christmas lights are the biggest, so tap Thanksgiving and Christmas Lights.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.299
Halloween, New Year, and other seasons are in the same list.

00:01:22.129 --> 00:01:23.992
You can also search for a city.

00:01:23.992 --> 00:01:26.287
Type its name, and tap it in the list.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.698
The map jumps there, which is handy if you are planning a trip to a different part of Southern California.

00:01:35.548 --> 00:01:39.148
To drive to what you see, tap Route these, the button on the map.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:45.790
On a computer it sits in the top bar, and on a phone, just above the list.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:48.613
It puts the places in view into driving order.

00:01:48.613 --> 00:01:53.223
It works for up to forty five places at a time, so zoom in if there are more.

00:01:54.074 --> 00:02:00.250
Your maps app can only take about nine stops at a time, so a long list is split into routes of nine or fewer.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:04.250
Each route starts where the last one ended, so you do not have to plan the break.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.049
Each route opens in Apple Maps, Google Maps, or Waze.

00:02:08.049 --> 00:02:12.425
On a phone, the site asks which one the first time, and remembers your answer.

00:02:12.425 --> 00:02:16.568
And you can tick off places as you go, so you can pick up where you left off.

00:02:16.568 --> 00:02:17.948
Tap the X to close.

00:02:19.503 --> 00:02:21.461
To narrow the list, tap Filters.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:34.727
You can pick a date, like tonight or this weekend, a city and how far from it to look, a county, free or ticketed, a type, such as home displays, and whether you can walk by a home.

00:02:34.727 --> 00:02:37.086
Use it when the list is too long to scan.

00:02:37.942 --> 00:02:40.159
That is how you find lights near you.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:43.969
Allow your location, so distances and directions work.

00:02:43.969 --> 00:02:44.996
Pick a season.

00:02:44.996 --> 00:02:46.012
Search a city.

00:02:46.012 --> 00:02:48.226
And narrow the list with Filters.

00:02:48.226 --> 00:02:50.530
In the next video, we will plan a whole night.

00:02:51.894 --> 00:02:53.390
Next up: Plan a night.
